void sdb_edit(procinfo *pi)
{
  char * filename = omStrDup("/tmp/sd000000");
  sprintf(filename+7,"%d",getpid());
  FILE *fp=fopen(filename,"w");
  if (fp==NULL)
  {
    Print("cannot open %s\n",filename);
    omFree(filename);
    return;
  }
  if (pi->language!= LANG_SINGULAR)
  {
    Print("cannot edit type %d\n",pi->language);
    fclose(fp);
    fp=NULL;
  }
  else
  {
    const char *editor=getenv("EDITOR");
    if (editor==NULL)
      editor=getenv("VISUAL");
    if (editor==NULL)
      editor="vi";
    editor=omStrDup(editor);

    if (pi->data.s.body==NULL)
    {
      iiGetLibProcBuffer(pi);
      if (pi->data.s.body==NULL)
      {
        PrintS("cannot get the procedure body\n");
        fclose(fp);
        si_unlink(filename);
        omFree(filename);
        return;
      }
    }

    fwrite(pi->data.s.body,1,strlen(pi->data.s.body),fp);
    fclose(fp);

    int pid=fork();
    if (pid!=0)
    {
      si_wait(&pid);
    }
    else if(pid==0)
    {
      if (strchr(editor,' ')==NULL)
      {
        execlp(editor,editor,filename,NULL);
        Print("cannot exec %s\n",editor);
      }
      else
      {
        char *p=(char *)omAlloc(strlen(editor)+strlen(filename)+2);
        sprintf(p,"%s %s",editor,filename);
        system(p);
      }
      exit(0);
    }
    else
    {
      PrintS("cannot fork\n");
    }

    fp=fopen(filename,"r");
    if (fp==NULL)
    {
      Print("cannot read from %s\n",filename);
    }
    else
    {
      fseek(fp,0L,SEEK_END);
      long len=ftell(fp);
      fseek(fp,0L,SEEK_SET);

      omFree((ADDRESS)pi->data.s.body);
      pi->data.s.body=(char *)omAlloc((int)len+1);
      myfread( pi->data.s.body, len, 1, fp);
      pi->data.s.body[len]='\0';
      fclose(fp);
    }
  }
  si_unlink(filename);
  omFree(filename);
}

raw_output:
1. The function allocates a fixed‑size string `filename` by duplicating the literal `"/tmp/sd000000"` (13 bytes including the terminating null).  
2. It then writes the process ID into the buffer with `sprintf(filename+7,"%d",getpid());`. The write starts at offset 7 and assumes the PID will fit into the remaining six bytes. If the PID contains more than five digits (e.g., on systems with large PIDs), `sprintf` will overflow the allocated buffer, corrupting adjacent memory. This is a classic stack/heap overflow (CWE‑122).  
3. The temporary file name is predictable (`/tmp/sdXXXXX`). An attacker can pre‑create a symbolic link with that name pointing to a sensitive file before the program runs. The program opens the file with `fopen(filename,"w")` and later reads it, enabling a TOCTOU symlink attack (CWE‑367).  
4. The editor command is taken directly from the environment (`EDITOR` or `VISUAL`). If the value contains spaces, the code builds a command string `p` with `sprintf(p,"%s %s",editor,filename);` and executes it via `system(p);`. Because the whole string is passed to the shell, an attacker can inject additional shell commands through a crafted `EDITOR` value (e.g., `EDITOR="vim; rm -rf /"`), leading to command injection (CWE‑78).  
5. When `editor` contains no spaces, the code calls `execlp(editor,editor,filename,NULL);`. Although `execlp` does not invoke a shell, the same unvalidated `EDITOR` value may point to an unintended executable, but the primary risk is the `system` path described above.  
6. The temporary file is opened, written, closed, then reopened for reading without any file permission checks or exclusive creation (`O_EXCL`). Combined with the predictable name, this exacerbates the symlink/TOCTOU risk.  
7. No authentication or authorization checks are performed; the function assumes it runs with appropriate privileges. If the process runs with elevated rights (e.g., set‑uid root), the aforementioned weaknesses can be exploited to gain higher privileges.  

Overall, the code exhibits multiple security flaws: a potential buffer overflow, a predictable temporary file leading to symlink attacks, and command injection via an untrusted environment variable.

